To further reduce latency, restructure operations within each column to maximize parallel computation of intermediate values and state updates. This reduces dependency chains within each column processing.

```c
static void MixColumns_hls(state_t state) {
  uint8_t i;
  #pragma HLS unroll yes
  for (i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
    uint8_t t = state[i][0];
    uint8_t Tmp = state[i][0] ^ state[i][1] ^ state[i][2] ^ state[i][3];
    
    // Parallel computation of all transformation factors
    uint8_t Tm0 = xtime(state[i][0] ^ state[i][1]);
    uint8_t Tm1 = xtime(state[i][1] ^ state[i][2]);
    uint8_t Tm2 = xtime(state[i][2] ^ state[i][3]);
    uint8_t Tm3 = xtime(state[i][3] ^ t);

    // Parallel state updates
    state[i][0] ^= Tm0 ^ Tmp;
    state[i][1] ^= Tm1 ^ Tmp;
    state[i][2] ^= Tm2 ^ Tmp;
    state[i][3] ^= Tm3 ^ Tmp;
  }
}

static void MixColumns(state_t *state) {
  MixColumns_hls(*state);
}
```

**Key Improvements:**  
1. **Explicit Parallelism:** Separates Tm calculations from state updates to break sequential dependencies
2. **Temporary Variables:** Uses dedicated registers for each transformation factor (Tm0-Tm3)
3. **Scheduling Freedom:** Allows HLS tool to schedule all xtime calls and state updates in parallel
4. **Maintained Full Unrolling:** Keeps `#pragma HLS unroll yes` for column-level parallelism

This version reduces intra-column dependencies while maintaining the same mathematical operations. The HLS tool can now better exploit spatial parallelism within each column's transformations.
